     "excerpt": "# On-Demand Skills for Software Engineering in the Age of AI: Packaging Expertise Your Agent Loads When It Needs It\n\n## Why On-Demand Skills Exist: The Context Problem\n\nThe usual first move with a coding agent is to put everything in the system prompt or a rule file: style guides, test conventions, security checklists, deployment steps. It works at first, then degrades in three ways. These are common engineering observations, not findings from the sources below.\n\n- **Context bloat:** Every task pays for every rule, relevant or not. Tokens spent on Terraform conventions are wasted on a CSS fix, and they crowd out the code and tool output the agent needs.\n- **Instruction conflicts:** Rules written by different people at different times contradict each other (\"always add tests\" vs. \"keep diffs minimal\"). The model has to guess which one wins.\n- **Degraded adherence:** The longer the…"
