     "file": "20261013-172341_explainer_how_langgraph_checkpointers_work_sqlite.md",
     "excerpt": "# How LangGraph Checkpointers Work: SQLite vs Postgres, and Resuming a Failed Run\n\n## What a checkpointer actually does\n\nA checkpointer is a persistence layer the graph runtime writes to while it executes. It saves state snapshots keyed by a thread, which is what makes a run inspectable and resumable.\n\n### Threads, checkpoints, and parent configs\n\n- **`thread_id`** is the key you pass in `config[\"configurable\"]`. It identifies one logical run or conversation. All of its history is stored under that ID.\n- **Checkpoint** is one saved snapshot of the graph state within a thread. Each has its own `checkpoint_id`.\n- **Parent config** is a pointer from a checkpoint to the one before it. `SqliteSaver`, for example, stores each checkpoint tied to its config and its parent config, and lists them newest first…"
